* burial chamber: Length approximately 3.6 meters, width approximately 1.8 meters, height approximately 1.8 meters

The chamber is meticulously crafted using large cut stones, with single slabs of granite from Heguri Town's Koshikizuka area (near the typeer site of Sekitoko Shrine) used for the back wall, side walls, and ceiling<ref name="ExplanationBoard"/>. The seams between the stones are sealed with plaster, and the floor is covered with gravel during the {{ill|modern period|ja|近世|12=draft}}<ref name="HeguriTown"/>. The ceiling stone of the passage was cut to match the slope of the mound, similar to the {{ill|Iwayayama Kofun|ja|岩屋山古墳|12=draft}} (Asuka Village){{Sfn|西宮古墳（平凡社）|1981}}. The floor of the chamber aligns with the terrace of the second tier of the mound, with the passage entrance lowered to type a pathway{{Sfn|西宮古墳（続古墳）|2002}}.

Inside the chamber lies a hollowed-out, house-shaped stone coffin made of layered {{ill|hyaloclastite|ja|ハイアロクラスタイト|12=draft}} (Ryuzan Stone). Currently, only the coffin body remains<ref name="ExplanationBoard"/>. The coffin measures 2.24 meters in length, 1.15 meters in width, and 0.76 meters in height<ref name="ExplanationBoard"/>, and features belt-shaped bands carved into its outer surface<ref name="HeguriTown">[http://www.town.heguri.nara.jp/web/kankou/bunkazai/bunkazai04-28.html Nishinomiya Kofun] (Heguri Town Homepage).</ref>.
